From 7aa613faf82349d5c4b0f3b70c1a70253d02ac0f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 22:20:31 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 210/211] dos: patch 0210 -- SDL_DOS_PACK24: present a 32-bit
 surface through a 24-bpp mode, packing 4->3 bytes

Why not just what: on an ATI Mach64 under UniVBE (dosags HW-486-66, probe
BW32 2026-09-24), CPU writes to the 640x480x32 LFB run at 7.7 MB/s, but
to the 640x480x24 LFB at 27.2 MB/s, with the same 8:8:8 colour. A 32-bpp
pixel costs about 0.52 us to put on screen; a 24-bpp one about 0.11 us.
AGS Quest forced to 32 bpp spends 11.5 ms per present at 32 bpp, and its
skipped frames are spikes of ~108k pushed px. A port that renders 32-bit
can keep its surface and show it through the 24-bpp mode.

What, behind SDL_DOS_PACK24=1 (off by default; with it unset the mode
list and every present are byte-unchanged):
- Modes: a 24-bpp direct-colour LFB mode whose masks are R16/G8/B0
  (memory B,G,R -- XRGB8888 without its X byte) is offered to the
  application as SDL_PIXELFORMAT_XRGB8888. The native 32-bpp mode of the
  same size is hidden, so a request for 32 bpp lands on it. Any other
  layout is left alone. The mode dump now logs each mode's masks.
- Direct-FB push: each dirty row is packed straight into the LFB, three
  dword stores per four pixels. That drops the X byte and nothing else,
  so the displayed RGB equals the 32-bit path's. The banked window packs
  into a row buffer and uses the existing banked copy.
- Non-direct path: its LFB surface gets the real BGR24 format, so SDL's
  own blit converts (slower, still correct).
- RUNMANI-visible lines: `DOS-PORT pack24 engaged mode= ... path=` at
  first use, `DOS-PORT pack24 active=1 mode= pushed_px=` at exit.

Lab evidence: dosags LAB-S32.md 9 (G2/G2b). The read-back verifier and
the rect-list hash used there are lab-only and live in patch 0211, not
here.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
